 ### Concentration of Color Weights Metric
-The Concentration of Color Weights Metric quantifies surface compactness by computing the weighted variance of depth samples along camera rays [S2, unofficial]. The procedure samples $N=36$ equally-spaced points within near/far bounds $[t_n, t_f]$, calculates corresponding color weights $w_i$, computes the weighted mean depth $\bar{t} = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^N w_i t_i}{\sum_{i=1}^N w_i}$, and computes the weighted variance $\Sigma_{t_i} = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^N w_i (t_i - \bar{t})^2}{N-1}$ [S2, unofficial]. The metric is averaged over 1000 randomly synthesized images at 256×256 resolution [S2, unofficial].
+The Concentration of Color Weights Metric quantifies surface compactness by computing the weighted variance of depth samples along camera rays [S2, unofficial]. It is computed by sampling $N=36$ equally-spaced points within near/far bounds $[t_n, t_f]$, calculating corresponding color weights $w_i$, computing the weighted mean depth $\bar{t} = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^N w_i t_i}{\sum_{i=1}^N w_i}$, and computing the weighted variance $\Sigma_{t_i} = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^N w_i (t_i - \bar{t})^2}{N-1}$ [S2, unofficial]. The metric is averaged over 1000 randomly synthesized images at 256×256 resolution [S2, unofficial]. A smaller variance implies a more compact learned surface [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Adds a linear pass over the samples along each ray to compute variance terms [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** No additional memory structures required beyond storing intermediate sample values [G].
-*   **Strengths:** Effectively reduces ambiguity in low-texture and few-shot regions common in indoor scenes [S1]. Encourages compact geometry by penalizing spread in weight and color distributions [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of loss weights ($\lambda_d, \lambda_w, \lambda_c$) and view coverage adjustment ($\lambda(\mathbf{r})$) [S1]. Performance depends on the quality of the pseudo-depth from the geometry scaffold [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** When optimizing neural radiance fields for indoor scenes with low-texture areas or sparse input views, particularly when using a geometry scaffold [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When computational budget is extremely tight and the scene is well-textured with dense views, where photometric loss may suffice [G].
